---
// Cedar Peak case study. Every number and quote carries a source label (README, SPEC,
// IDEATION, CALL, PDA, PRICING, DEMO). The label to file map is kept outside the repo,
// because the source paths carry the real client's name.
import CaseSection from "../../components/case/CaseSection.astro";
import Shot from "../../components/case/Shot.astro";
import Stats from "../../components/case/Stats.astro";
import Quote from "../../components/case/Quote.astro";
import Decision from "../../components/case/Decision.astro";
import costSheet from "../../assets/work/cedar-peak/cost-sheet-check.png";
import closingPackage from "../../assets/work/cedar-peak/closing-package-check.png";
import board from "../../assets/work/cedar-peak/pipeline-board.png";
import stuck from "../../assets/work/cedar-peak/stuck-deals.png";
---

<CaseSection id="context" standfirst="A closing team of three turns the field's deal paperwork into closed home sales.">
  <p>
    The client operates manufactured housing communities across many states. When a home sells, the community manager
    on site sends a cost sheet for the deal, then a closing package of signed documents. A contract and closing team of
    three checks every document, records the deal in an Excel sales tracker and in the property system, and closes it.
    Closed deals then feed commissions, which go to the COO for approval and to HR for payroll.
  </p>
  <dl class="users">
    <div><dt>Contract and closing team</dt><dd>Three people who check, record and close every deal.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Community managers</dt><dd>Submit cost sheets and closing packages from the field.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>COO, HR and payroll</dt><dd>Approve and pay the commissions that closed deals earn.</dd></div>
  </dl>
  <p>
    This case covers the two modules I built inside a seven-solution, thirty-one-screen prototype that walked the
    client's teams through what an AI audit had recommended for them.
  </p>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection
  id="problem"
  standfirst="Every deal entered twice by hand, most closing packages wrong on arrival, and commission checks on the critical path to payroll."
>
  <p>
    Errors surfaced late. The closing team found them when it opened each package, and some only when commissions were
    checked against every cost sheet at the end of the month. Lot numbers on incoming cost sheets often failed to match
    the property system, down to a trailing space, so each mismatch meant a manual lookup before the deal could move.
  </p>
  <Stats
    items={[
      { value: "300 to 350", label: "closed deals a month, each typed by hand into the sales tracker and the property system", source: "SPEC:11,25" },
      { value: "60 to 70%", label: "of closing packages arrive from the field with errors, confirmed separately by all three team members", source: "SPEC:35" },
      { value: "A full day", label: "each month to check commissions for the most complex region, where one manager can appear on up to 67 deals", source: "SPEC:47" },
    ]}
  />
  <Quote by="The sales closing and compliance manager" where="discovery call, March 2026" source="CALL:102">
    Maybe no signature, maybe the lot amount is not listed on the lease, maybe we don't have the concession addendum
    ... and then we cannot move forward until we get those revised documents.
  </Quote>
  <p>
    Each returned package waits for the buyer to come back and sign again, which is outside the team's control. And
    commissions sit on the critical path to payroll: a slow check delays the COO's approval and the second payroll of
    the month.
  </p>
  <Quote by="The sales closing and compliance manager" where="discovery call, March 2026" source="CALL:199">
    That's very manual, and it can take us, like, a full day to do just [one operator's] commission calculations.
  </Quote>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection
  id="research"
  standfirst="Pre-call questionnaires, a discovery call with screen shares, and live walkthroughs of the prototype."
>
  <p>
    The audit ran department by department. Before each call, team members answered a questionnaire. On the call, they
    shared their screens and walked through the real work.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      I was on the closing team's call. The manager walked us through the sales tracker, a cost sheet, a closing package
      and the commission worksheets on screen, including checking an instalment payment with a desk calculator.
    </li>
    <li>
      All three team members had named the closing package error rate in their questionnaires before the call, and a
      coordinator raised commission checks independently.
    </li>
    <li>
      I turned the department transcripts into ranked pain points and the solution designs the screens follow.
    </li>
    <li>The client's teams then saw the prototype in live walkthroughs.</li>
  </ul>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection
  id="decisions"
  standfirst="Catch errors where they start, explain every check, show only the exceptions, and leave every approval with a person."
>
  <Decision title="Catch errors at the source">
    <p>
      The cost sheet is checked the moment the field submits it, and the closing package is checked before close, so
      mistakes stop before they reach commissions. The field sees a failure while the deal is still in front of them,
      not days later when the package comes back.
    </p>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="Every check explains itself">
    <p>
      A failed check names the field, the rule and the fix: the VIN needs 17 characters and has 15, or the lot does not
      exist in that community. The person who made the mistake can see what to fix.
    </p>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="Exceptions first">
    <p>
      The pipeline holds every deal, but the stuck deals report lists only those past the expected time for their stage,
      sorted by days stuck. The team works the exceptions, not the whole list.
    </p>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="Approvals stay with people">
    <p>
      The checks never approve anything. A clean cost sheet moves on to closing review. A closing package with gaps can
      only go back to the field: approval stays switched off until it is clean. Commissions still move through closing
      review, the COO, HR and payroll.
    </p>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="A no-code field checklist" notBuilt>
    <p>
      The audit's quick win for this problem was a pre-submission checklist for the field, which needed no development.
      I chose to build validation into submission instead.
    </p>
    <p>
      A checklist relies on the field to fill it in, and the audit itself named field adoption as its friction point. It
      also cannot see the property system, where lot numbers on incoming cost sheets often fail to match.
    </p>
  </Decision>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection
  id="solution"
  standfirst="Field validation, with cost sheet and closing package checks, and a sales pipeline, with a board, deal detail and reports."
>
  <p>
    Both modules run in the browser on invented mock data and connect to nothing. The prototype's job was to walk the
    client's teams through what the audit had recommended for them.
  </p>
  <Shot
    n={1}
    src={costSheet}
    alt="Cost sheet validation screen. A deal cost sheet on the left; on the right, seven checks, three failed: the lot is not found in the community, the VIN is 15 characters instead of 17, and the senior sign-off is missing."
    caption="Cost sheet check at submission, failing example. Three of seven checks fail, and each says why."
    notes={[
      { x: 61, y: 27.6, lx: 64.8, text: "Checks run against the system of record: the lot on the sheet must exist in the property system for that community." },
      { x: 61, y: 42.7, lx: 64.8, text: "A failure says exactly what is wrong: the VIN needs 17 characters and has 15." },
      { x: 61, y: 71, lx: 64.8, text: "A missing sign-off is caught before the sheet leaves the field." },
      { x: 61, y: 80.6, lx: 64.8, text: "Nothing moves to closing review until every check passes. Each failed check opens to a suggested fix." },
    ]}
  />
  <Shot
    n={2}
    src={closingPackage}
    alt="Closing package validation results. Five of seven required documents are present, with the bill of sale and disclosure statement missing. A field-by-field comparison shows the contract price does not match the cost sheet. Send back to field is active and approve is disabled."
    caption="Closing package check before close. Two documents are missing and one price does not match the cost sheet."
    notes={[
      { x: 92.5, y: 12.4, text: "Missing documents are named, with why each is required." },
      { x: 93.8, y: 61.8, text: "Each field read from the documents is compared with the cost sheet." },
      { x: 3.2, y: 76.1, text: "The mismatch explains itself: which value, from which document, and by how much." },
      { x: 58.4, y: 93.1, text: "With gaps open, the only way forward is back to the field. Approval stays switched off." },
    ]}
  />
  <Shot
    n={3}
    src={board}
    alt="Sales pipeline board. Summary counts across the top, including stuck deals, then filters, then deal cards in stage columns from cost sheet received to closed, each showing days in stage."
    caption="Pipeline board. Every deal in one place, in place of the spreadsheet tracker."
    notes={[
      { x: 1.6, y: 58, text: "Each deal sits in one stage, from cost sheet received through to title transfer." },
      { x: 14, y: 77.3, lx: 16.6, text: "Each card shows how long the deal has sat in its stage." },
      { x: 76, y: 12.6, text: "Stuck deals are counted at the top, beside the month's progress." },
    ]}
  />
  <Shot
    n={4}
    src={stuck}
    alt="Stuck deals report listing three deals, each with days in stage, the stage threshold of seven days, and a flag showing how many days over."
    caption="Stuck deals report. Only the exceptions, sorted by days stuck."
    notes={[
      { x: 3.1, y: 42.8, text: "Only deals past the expected time for their stage appear here." },
      { x: 96.6, y: 59.3, text: "Each flag carries its reason: days in stage against the stage's threshold." },
    ]}
  />
  <p>
    The reports also replace the email of pending deals the closing team wrote to the field by hand each morning.
  </p>
  <h3>How it was built</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Front end only. Every screen runs on typed mock data, and nothing is connected to a real system.</li>
    <li>Built with Claude Code over four days. Every commit in the original history was co-authored with Claude.</li>
    <li>Next.js 16 (App Router), React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS 4, Recharts and Framer Motion.</li>
    <li>
      Anonymised for the public copy: the operator, its people, communities and figures are invented.
      <a href="https://github.com/kakkiris/mhc-operations-ai-demo">The code is on GitHub</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection id="outcome" standfirst="My pipeline module became the first milestone of a signed six-figure product build.">
  <p>
    After the walkthroughs, the client signed a five-month product build: eleven modules in two solution streams,
    delivered in five monthly milestones. Module 1, the home sales pipeline, ships in the first milestone. Commission
    calculation and exception routing is Module 4.
  </p>
  <Stats
    items={[
      { value: "Module 1", label: "the home sales pipeline, delivered in the first milestone", source: "PDA:5" },
      { value: "11 modules", label: "in two solution streams", source: "PDA:4" },
      { value: "5 months", label: "of build, one milestone each month", source: "PDA:3.2,5" },
    ]}
  />
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection id="learned" standfirst="A check that names its own fix beats one that only blocks.">
  <p>
    A check that names its own fix is worth more than one that only blocks, because the person who made the mistake
    can correct it where it started.
  </p>
  <p>
    The cheapest fix in an audit is not always the right build. The checklist needed no code, but the errors that
    mattered sat in data the field could not see.
  </p>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection id="role" standfirst="Two people built the prototype. I built two of its modules and worked on the audit behind it.">
  <div class="readme">
    <p>
      Two people built this app. I built the field validation and sales pipeline modules: the deal cost sheet check, the
      closing package check, the pipeline board, deal detail, and reports. I also worked on the audit behind the suite,
      turning department discovery transcripts into ranked pain points and the solution designs these screens follow.
      My colleague built the app shell and the other modules.
    </p>
    <p class="note">From the repository README, word for word.</p>
  </div>
</CaseSection>
